    "result": "I implemented `search_products` in `solution.py` and wrote a 57-test pytest suite in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` passes. The query is a single parameterized SELECT chosen from four fixed SQL strings keyed by `(order_by, descending)`, so no caller text is ever spliced into SQL. Name matching uses `instr(lower(name), lower(?))`, which avoids LIKE and its wildcards, so `%` and `_` are literal. Validation rejects bools and floats for `limit` and `offset`, and it raises `ValueError` for a bad `order_by`. Ties are broken by `id` ascending in both directions. I corrected several of my own hand-computed expected orderings before the first run. I did not run coverage, mutation, CRAP or bandit checks, since only the pytest command was allowed, so those gates are unverified.",
